#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097783} :activity/id #uuid "00c1cd54-a7af-5240-860b-4432978fec0c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/id #uuid "7fcc3a06-676a-55a2-9979-d3726053babe" :topic/math-academy-id 1108} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097807 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{′}(a) & ={lim}_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{′}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{′}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "d34fc877-5572-53a2-b957-2c5d44a488f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13330 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "9ad2def5-511c-587f-9847-fa7af8e06405" :step/math-academy-id 466057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097872 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like \n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3,\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}g}({g}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+2x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6{g}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(x) = 1+2x$ for $g,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6(1+2x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" :tutorial/math-academy-id 415 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a2d483f7-5abb-5070-9d8d-2db12d3f0358" :step/math-academy-id 773 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525021}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "7f46eb68-796d-5c0d-a89b-b1dbdf45d975" :step/math-academy-id 774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097785}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557259}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "60f88dd0-c1bd-546f-9dac-f440401fd02b" :step/math-academy-id 777 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097873 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58c36c82-b739-55f9-8e99-4df1e0cbde24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 418 :tutorial/title "Another Notation for the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "90637184-1911-5cfb-bf2c-dbdad69086a3" :step/math-academy-id 780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097787}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493336} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557301}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"} :step/id #uuid "35489675-a0a1-59ea-b1f7-ab794d855f54" :step/math-academy-id 781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097750}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096888}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520608} :activity/id #uuid "63195741-23b2-5a66-a987-8d1b10be7716" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/id #uuid "7fcc3a06-676a-55a2-9979-d3726053babe" :topic/math-academy-id 1108} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e10ad36-36ec-5632-8f07-eff850b3becf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x^{3}-9x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520153} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b7fd03c-605a-5e8e-8867-8d3e258ab8cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "930f9486-b8c5-5ccf-b917-a4806acde6bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-314207" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})^{′}=4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})(\\,4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "35b3efb8-a2d4-59c0-b538-ee4b244189e0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520543 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf96aed-c4c2-50a0-9312-269557ced95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8(1-3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520029} {:answer/id #uuid "4793072d-d61e-5f93-bb0c-993627ee72c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24(1+3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520100} {:answer/id #uuid "99709777-eb56-5d41-ac58-1c43410ac856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24(1-3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520209} {:answer/id #uuid "d2298536-2087-59b8-a435-384b956031dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24(1+3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520286} {:answer/id #uuid "e7514498-fddf-54d8-9478-ff10ceb72766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3(1-3x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520330}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520209} :answer-field/id #uuid "44da7608-9cb4-57b2-9694-53ee352f7833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73597027-954f-5bff-8c64-de058a3fe822" :question/math-academy-id "q-107296" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(1-3x)}^{8},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{8}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{8})^{′}=8{g}^{7}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =8{g}^{7}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-24(1-3x)^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4350c72e-f9bd-5210-90fd-d37e9cc293c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c7c493-bcdb-5703-aa16-7951eec23ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520068} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b694189-fec1-5a06-8456-a524e215acf6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa666076-05a3-50c7-be3d-b7a39d6d4f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-314192" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+1}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}})|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(0)+1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "51f59503-df73-54cc-af64-3dcb163fe7d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454a250f-2e0e-5eff-ad08-2e1a4569c3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520091} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf36f6a-ac30-5d76-9499-88f79530ef58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520108} {:answer/id #uuid "a63c2527-a87a-5317-bb52-435b49980a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520218} {:answer/id #uuid "ceae46e6-5020-51b1-a35e-422b64ddda99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520281} {:answer/id #uuid "edcc6c31-a212-5340-8bdb-e1b0f8c22746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520091} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e463a5a-0bf7-5c15-8a84-e8fb513aab4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "98cde390-f268-5ffd-9f2f-31f016a0c432" :question/math-academy-id "q-107303" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{2x-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "828caf19-ec46-5c3a-829e-707d043329c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520551}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd0f410-6e9b-5a13-84b6-2793313eb013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-3}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520005} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0c3af3-091e-560d-ac8e-abe55cc9d0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520009} {:answer/id #uuid "75d7df1e-163d-51cf-9003-0d07b95639d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520161} {:answer/id #uuid "86d89cd3-8e55-5f29-83ed-c409f606d553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520189} {:answer/id #uuid "996b8fd1-b20a-565b-98d1-c22e6ee7d68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520009} :answer-field/id #uuid "686cff4f-8265-5ba3-8560-bcdf5671d8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "321f22b7-0ca5-594c-82a7-c5cf5cf23fad" :question/math-academy-id "q-71952" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dcb12c9b-27b2-535b-bf95-11fa19d50067" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520543}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519990}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542258} :activity/id #uuid "672652bb-f97d-53e5-ac91-2daa74826e74" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/id #uuid "7fcc3a06-676a-55a2-9979-d3726053babe" :topic/math-academy-id 1108} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc8b5aee-000a-5a8d-818f-e20c5debd3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316184} {:answer/id #uuid "7c419ad2-83cf-57c7-b5a2-d7c1c6a8cf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541677} :answer-field/id #uuid "625a79d7-54d7-570b-aa7c-729d582ac660" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ebc59fe-5ccc-59b1-9757-9c760459c2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-342804" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(4x+3)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=4x+3$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5})^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+3)^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+3)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "262d4a97-2a24-516f-befc-4aec82c20974"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365d6570-d8cb-539f-a88f-8f59a136deaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{145}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541535} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e65a94-bc73-5511-b4e0-737b7fd4a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541771} {:answer/id #uuid "ba942dc9-148d-5089-895a-5986590e89c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541806} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa31f6a-0231-55ef-b7c1-eda435cc6895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541897} {:answer/id #uuid "fb45a9d2-d3cd-5774-a7e6-b9653552ab14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541806} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bb9c7f-0a51-5ad4-a60d-65bfb06add50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed421aa6-2e1d-544f-a145-c01b981f6e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-20181" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-3}+3{x}^{3}$ at the point where $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-3}.$ We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-3.$ Then $f=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}+9{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4-3}}+9(4)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+144 \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "68e702e2-55d1-5021-9663-e37aa362023f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496974f1-cd7b-5abe-b03b-8caf3c2ef7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541574} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df6c47b-c813-591d-800c-7da989a956a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "316d8b52-05f5-5660-9987-6f2357a674ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-342789" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(1)+2}{2\\sqrt{-{1}^{2}+2(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dd09ffd1-587d-547b-937b-c610cc67ad37" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542241}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541420}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557366} :activity/id #uuid "eefeaacd-681e-5870-8db3-088073475fd0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/id #uuid "7fcc3a06-676a-55a2-9979-d3726053babe" :topic/math-academy-id 1108} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557366 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0576b7ba-7b60-5cde-90eb-b7b6e9fa9cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(2x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556443} {:answer/id #uuid "13af737a-117c-5f32-93ba-e6c9200ca3d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12(x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556472} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb99734-0a8c-580d-a0f8-4954f321ea9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6(2x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556506} {:answer/id #uuid "97c20d2c-d45f-5b09-9f39-ebe08248b75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12(2x-11)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556794} {:answer/id #uuid "a776abf8-3706-5639-a382-e250cca33636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12(2x-11)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556844}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556844} :answer-field/id #uuid "9164d315-5789-5468-b831-e821976b6b32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b4d3073-0a5e-5d32-b8ba-a307535b4915" :question/math-academy-id "q-71958" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2x-11)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2x-11$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2x-11)^{′}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12(2x-11)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ee4acf2-5857-55f8-bb3a-ded7762c8061" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557409}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c368e94-1966-5731-867b-0babe7d75b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36(6x-5)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556637} :answer-field/id #uuid "258869c8-85b7-5a80-8cff-298b4785a0ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1385fb4-0635-5e4c-a92d-2175e82b7bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-314205" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(6x-5)}^{6},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=6x-5$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{6}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{6})^{′}=6{g}^{5}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(6x-5)^{′}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =36(6x-5)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cca24e3e-cdea-5e7c-b3c8-5f84dfb83ea6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49385517-eeac-5b31-adab-df094762881b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556593} {:answer/id #uuid "604e0e8a-a825-50c3-a298-9ae13e709a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556648} {:answer/id #uuid "9404be8d-cb6c-53b2-8a36-eb362e775360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556786} {:answer/id #uuid "95cb350d-4388-53ea-9992-f43b19639582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556791} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3dc5f8-2374-5afe-8f07-96ed5f7a5da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556593} :answer-field/id #uuid "add9725c-6d77-5248-a0ae-02fe439fa683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "67f07fb8-cc06-5cda-9a1f-cc029f68242d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107288" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}})|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(4)+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc153cbc-8b84-5906-bc61-6b445d0eb4ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557395}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556425}]}
